{% doc %}
  blocks/_featured-pills-check.liquid

  One line of a list that is read as a set of promises: a mark, a short label,
  and an optional sentence under it. Five of them stacked are the ingredient
  list in the *Our mission* panel; four are the philosophy list in *Nutrition
  facts*.

  ## Why it is not `blocks/icon-with-text.liquid`

  Icon with text is a glyph beside a sentence, and its job is to sit anywhere —
  a row of three under a hero, a column in a footer. It has no idea of being one
  of a set.

  This one does, and that is the whole difference. A list like this says two
  things at once: here are five facts, *and* this is the one you are looking at.
  So the row carries a surface of its own that changes with **Highlight this
  row** — a different fill behind the mark, a different background, a drawn
  border — and the sentence under the label appears only where a row is worth
  expanding on. Building that out of a Group with an Icon with text inside it is
  four blocks per row and twenty for the list, none of which agree with each
  other by construction.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a row with nothing to be a row of
  is not a block. It is addable inside a **Pill** and nowhere else.

  ## The mark is the theme's

  Shape, Ring and the icon colours are `snippets/icon-mark.liquid`: the same
  three groups with the same ids that the Feature icon, the Highlight tile, the
  Accordion row and the footer's Utility item offer. A merchant who has drawn
  one mark in this theme has drawn this one, and a change to how a dotted ring
  is stroked reaches all of them.

  What is added on top is one pair: the fill and the glyph colour the mark takes
  **while the row is highlighted**. They are settings rather than a hard-coded
  accent because the highlight is the design's loudest moment and a theme cannot
  guess a store's accent.

  ## The whole row can be a link, and it is not an `<a>`

  Same reason `snippets/group.liquid` gives: a row holds a label and a sentence
  and either could one day hold a link of its own, and an `<a>` inside an `<a>`
  is invalid markup that browsers recover from unpredictably. So the row stays a
  `<div>` and an absolutely positioned link covers it.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign highlighted = s.highlighted | default: false

  assign has_icon = false
  if s.icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the surface ----

    Two backgrounds, one of which is only ever painted while the row is the
    highlighted one. Both are `color_background`, so a merchant can hand either
    a gradient — which is what the drawn design's quiet rows are: a flat mint
    that is a gradient of one colour in Shopify's own picker.

    The alpha goes into the colour rather than onto the element, for the reason
    `blocks/card-content.liquid` sets out: `opacity` here would fade the label
    the surface exists to sit behind.
  endcomment
  assign surface_paint = s.background | default: ''
  if highlighted and s.background_highlight != blank
    assign surface_paint = s.background_highlight
  endif

  comment
    ---- the frame ----

    Resolved the way every border in this theme is: `none` emits nothing at all,
    and with no colour picked the line is `currentColor` faded by `color-mix`,
    because the colour filters cannot take an alpha channel from a keyword.

    The highlighted row may draw a line where a quiet one draws none — that is
    the drawn design — so the style is read from whichever of the two the row
    currently is, and a merchant who wants the same frame on both simply sets
    both to the same thing.
  endcomment
  assign border_line = s.border_style | default: 'none'
  if highlighted
    assign border_line = s.border_style_highlight | default: border_line
  endif

  assign border_width = 0
  if border_line != 'none'
    assign border_width = s.border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0

    assign border_percent = s.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    assign border_source = s.border_color
    if highlighted and s.border_color_highlight != blank
      assign border_source = s.border_color_highlight
    endif

    if border_source == blank
      assign border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign border_alpha = border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign border_paint = border_source | color_modify: 'alpha', border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  comment
    Two properties, not one — `flex` only means anything to a flex item, and a
    row is one only when the panel around it is a flex container. Fill is the
    default because a list of rows is a column of equal boxes; a row that hugs
    its own label is a ragged list.
  endcomment
  if s.width == 'fit'
    assign width_flex = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  else
    assign width_flex = '1 1 auto'
    assign width_size = '100%'
  endif

  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'
  assign label_classes = 'featured-pills-check__label block-text--' | append: preset
  if s.color == 'gradient'
    assign label_classes = label_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif

  assign classes = 'featured-pills-check icon-mark-host'
  if highlighted
    assign classes = classes | append: ' featured-pills-check--on'
  endif

  assign link_url = s.link | default: ''
-%}

{%- capture icon_mark_style -%}
  {%- render 'icon-mark', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Written after the snippet's own properties so they override them, and only
    while the row is the highlighted one. A row that is not highlighted emits
    neither, so the mark is exactly what `icon-mark` drew and nothing here has
    an opinion about it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if highlighted -%}
    {%- if s.highlight_fill != blank -%}--icon-mark-fill: {{ s.highlight_fill }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.highlight_icon_color != blank -%}--icon-mark-color: {{ s.highlight_icon_color }};{%- endif -%}
    {%- if s.highlight_ring_color != blank -%}--icon-mark-ring-color: {{ s.highlight_ring_color }};{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture label_style -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture row_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --fpc-flex: {{ width_flex }};
  --fpc-width: {{ width_size }};
  --fpc-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 14 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --fpc-stack-gap: {{ s.gap_vertical | default: 6 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --fpc-radius: {{ s.border_radius | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --fpc-border-width: {{ border_width }}px;
  {%- if border_width > 0 -%}
    --fpc-border-style: {{ border_line }};
    --fpc-border-color: {{ border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if surface_paint != '' -%}
    --fpc-background: {{ surface_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.text_color != blank -%}
    --fpc-text: {{ s.text_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.description_color != blank -%}
    --fpc-description-color: {{ s.description_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  --fpc-description-size: {{ s.description_size | default: 14 | plus: 0 | divided_by: 10.0 }}rem;
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ row_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- if has_icon -%}
    <span class="featured-pills-check__mark icon-mark" aria-hidden="true" style="{{ icon_mark_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
      {% render 'icon-mark', block: s %}
      {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}
    </span>
  {%- endif -%}

  <span class="featured-pills-check__body">
    {%- if s.label != blank -%}
      <span class="{{ label_classes }}" style="{{ label_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
        {{- s.label | escape -}}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The sentence under the label. A row that does not have one renders no
      element at all rather than an empty box the stack gap would still space
      away from the label.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if s.description != blank -%}
      <span class="featured-pills-check__text">{{ s.description }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </span>

  {%- if link_url != '' -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `aria-label` falls back to the row's own label, and then to the URL. A
      link announced as nothing at all is the one outcome worth ruling out.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a
      class="featured-pills-check__link"
      href="{{ link_url }}"
      {% if s.link_new_tab %}target="_blank" rel="noopener"{% endif %}
      aria-label="{{ s.link_label | default: s.label | default: link_url | escape }}"
    ></a>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% # theme-check-disable ExcessiveSettingsCount %}
{% schema %}
